---
import CalculatorLayout from '@/layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import DiscountCalculator from '@/components/calculators/DiscountCalculator';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How do I calculate a discount?',
    a: 'Multiply the original price by the discount percentage and divide by 100 to get the amount off, then subtract it. For $80 at 25% off: 80 × 25 ÷ 100 = $20 off, so you pay $60. Or multiply by the part you still pay: 80 × 0.75 = $60.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is 30% off plus an extra 20% off the same as 50% off?',
    a: 'No. The extra 20% is taken off the already-reduced price. On $100, 30% off gives $70, and 20% off $70 is $56. That’s 44% off in total, not 50%. The calculator shows the real combined discount when you add an extra discount.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I work out what percentage discount I got?',
    a: 'Divide the amount you saved by the original price and multiply by 100. If a $80 item costs $65, you saved $15, and 15 ÷ 80 × 100 = 18.75% off. Choose “Amount off” in the calculator to see this automatically.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I find the original price before a discount?',
    a: 'Divide the sale price by the part you paid. If you paid $60 after 25% off, you paid 75% of the original price, so the original was 60 ÷ 0.75 = $80.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is sales tax calculated before or after the discount?',
    a: 'In most places, tax is charged on the discounted price. Work out the sale price here, then add tax with the <a href="/sales-tax-calculator">sales tax calculator</a>.',
  },
];
---

<CalculatorLayout
  slug="discount-calculator"
  title="Discount Calculator – Sale Price & Percent Off"
  description="Free discount calculator. Find the sale price and how much you save with a percentage or fixed amount off, including stacked extra discounts."
  intro="Find the sale price and how much you save, whether it’s a percentage off, a fixed amount off or an extra discount on top of a sale."
  faqs={faqs}
  updated="2026-09-24"
>
  <DiscountCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="learn">
    <h2>How to use the discount calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Original price:</strong> the price before any discount.</li>
      <li><strong>Discount type:</strong> a percentage off (e.g. 25% off) or a fixed amount off (e.g. $15 off).</li>
      <li><strong>Extra discount</strong> (optional): for offers like “an extra 10% off sale items” that apply on top of the first discount.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Discount formulas</h2>
    <div class="formula">Sale price = Original price × (1 − Discount ÷ 100)</div>
    <div class="formula">Discount % = (Original − Sale price) ÷ Original × 100</div>
    <p>With an extra discount, apply each one in turn:</p>
    <div class="formula">Sale price = Original × (1 − D₁ ÷ 100) × (1 − D₂ ÷ 100)</div>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>
    <p>A jacket costs <strong>$80</strong> and is <strong>25% off</strong>: 80 × 0.25 = $20 off, so the sale price is <strong>$60</strong>.</p>

    <h3>Worked example: stacked discounts</h3>
    <p>
      A <strong>$100</strong> item is <strong>30% off</strong>, with an <strong>extra 20% off</strong> at checkout. The first discount brings it to $70. The extra 20% is taken off $70, which is
      $14, so you pay <strong>$56</strong>. In total that’s <strong>44% off</strong>, not the 50% you might expect.
    </p>

    <h2>Quick discount table</h2>
    <p>What you pay for a $100 item:</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Discount</th><th>You pay</th><th>Multiply the price by</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>10% off</td><td>$90</td><td>0.90</td></tr>
        <tr><td>20% off</td><td>$80</td><td>0.80</td></tr>
        <tr><td>25% off</td><td>$75</td><td>0.75</td></tr>
        <tr><td>30% off</td><td>$70</td><td>0.70</td></tr>
        <tr><td>50% off</td><td>$50</td><td>0.50</td></tr>
        <tr><td>30% + extra 20% off</td><td>$56</td><td>0.56</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Tips for spotting a real bargain</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Compare the final price,</strong> not the percentage: 40% off an inflated price can cost more than 20% off elsewhere.</li>
      <li><strong>Remember that stacked discounts don’t simply add up.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Check whether tax and delivery</strong> are added after the discount.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
